An Ansible playbook needs completion on the jump host, where a team member left off. Below are the details:
The inventory file /home/thor/ansible/inventory requires adjustments. The playbook must run on App Server 1 in Stratos DC. Update the inventory accordingly.
Create a playbook /home/thor/ansible/playbook.yml. Include a task to create an empty file /tmp/file.txt on App Server 1.

Note: Validation will run the playbook using the command ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml. Ensure the playbook works without any additional arguments.

SOLUTION
1. Go to the Ansible directory
cd /home/thor/ansible
2. Check the inventory
cat inventory

Set it so App Server 1 is in the inventory. In these KodeKloud labs, App Server 1 is typically stapp01.

A simple inventory is:

[app]
stapp01

If the existing inventory already contains the correct hostname/IP and credentials, keep those details and just ensure stapp01 belongs to a group used by the playbook.

3. Create playbook.yml
---
- name: Create file on App Server 1
  hosts: app
  become: yes


  tasks:
    - name: Create empty file
      ansible.builtin.file:
        path: /tmp/file.txt
        state: touch
4. Test the inventory
ansible -i inventory app -m ping

You should get:

stapp01 | SUCCESS => ...
5. Run exactly as the validator will
ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml

Then verify:

ansible -i inventory app -m shell -a "ls -l /tmp/file.txt"

You should see /tmp/file.txt.

Yes — your current inventory is pointing to App Server 3 (stapp03), but the task requires App Server 1.

Replace the inventory with stapp01.

cd /home/thor/ansible


cat > inventory <<'EOF'
[app]
stapp01 ansible_user=banner ansible_ssh_pass=$pwd ansible_ssh_common_args='-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no'
EOF

Then check it:

cat inventory

You should see:

[app]
stapp01 ansible_user=banner ansible_ssh_pass=$pwd ansible_ssh_common_args='-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no'

One important issue: $pwd is currently literal text in the inventory. If pwd is actually an environment variable containing the password, Ansible inventory does not expand $pwd automatically.

First test:

ansible -i inventory app -m ping


Important: The playbook uses hosts: app, so the inventory group must be named app. This also ensures the validator's exact command works without additional arguments.
